WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.040
Data exposure can happen when sensitive fields are stored in plaintext, sent without transport protection, logged carelessly, cached in unsafe places, or returned to clients that do not need them.

2
00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:19.893
Weak encryption is often a design and implementation problem rather than a single missing library call.

3
00:00:19.893 --> 00:00:30.803
Outdated algorithms, hard-coded secrets, custom crypto, poor randomness, and weak password hashing all reduce the protection that encryption is supposed to provide.

4
00:00:30.803 --> 00:00:39.319
Missing or misconfigured TLS leaves credentials, session tokens, and business data exposed to interception or downgrade attacks.

5
00:00:39.319 --> 00:00:46.505
Certificate validation, protocol versions, and secure defaults must be handled consistently across services.

6
00:00:46.505 --> 00:00:51.428
Key management is part of the control, not an administrative afterthought.

7
00:00:51.428 --> 00:00:59.278
Keys need protected storage, rotation, access limits, lifecycle management, and separation from the data they protect.

8
00:00:59.278 --> 00:01:03.736
Use strong, approved cryptographic algorithms and vetted libraries.

9
00:01:03.736 --> 00:01:07.262
Protect data in transit with properly configured TLS.

10
00:01:07.262 --> 00:01:13.183
Store keys in managed secret or key management systems with rotation and access controls.

11
00:01:13.183 --> 00:01:25.026
Selected NIST mappings for this topic include SC-12 - Cryptographic Key Establishment and Management; SC-13 - Cryptographic Protection; SC-28 - Protection of Information at Rest.

